Venetium 0061 - word-at-a-time memchr, memcmp, strchr, strcmp and strlen for Windows ARM32.
From the performance research (C:\handoff\PERF-RESEARCH.md, item S1), his "fix all".
The static CRT's ARM32 string routines are byte-at-a-time loops (libvcruntime memchr: ldrb/cmp/beq/adds/subs/bne per
byte; libucrt strlen: ldrsb/cmp/bne per byte; strchr and strcmp likewise), and libvcruntime memcmp drops to one byte per
iteration as soon as either pointer is odd. Blink's String::Find, V8's String.indexOf, URL and header parsing lean on
them. build/win/venetium_string_arm.c implements the five with 32-bit words (the exact zero-byte test
(v - 0x01010101) & ~v & 0x80808080, aligned loads; memcmp reads its second operand with unaligned single-word LDR, which
ARMv7 Windows allows). Same results as the CRT, including strcmp's -1/0/1 and memcmp's byte difference. memchr and
memcmp never read outside [p, p + n); the string functions may read up to 3 bytes past the NUL but only inside its
aligned word, so never into another page. Built with -fno-builtin (the compiler cannot turn the loops back into calls to
themselves) and without vectorization (plain 32-bit loads). //build/win:venetium_string_arm is a source_set added to
//build/config:common_deps for current_cpu == "arm", so its object is linked directly into every ARM32 binary and the
one-function CRT members (memchr.obj, memcmp.obj, strchr.obj in libvcruntime; strlen.obj, strcmp.obj in libucrt) are
never pulled in. Verified on the build host: the same source, renamed, against the host CRT on every alignment, length
and match position plus page-end reads against a guard page - 791,040,320 checks, 0 failures. memcpy/memset stay the
CRT's (they are already word/NEON based). x86 is unchanged.

